game, well below the ~120 norm); lean under on his rushing line.</p><p><strong>Courtland Sutton</strong> faces a tough cover \u2014 KC hold WR1s to just 50 yds/game (vs ~58); a rec-yards under lean.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p>In the red zone KC throw it (56% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Rashee Rice</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>KC \u2014 a <em>pass-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>KC stay aggressive through the air even with a lead \u2014 they pass <strong>50%</strong> on early downs when ahead versus the league's 44%. They don't sit on the ball, which keeps their receivers live in scripts where most offenses would be running out the clock.</p><p><strong>Kenneth Walker III</strong> (15 carries, 67 yards) projects to his baseline workload \u2014 no strong script push either way in a close game.</p><p><strong>Rashee Rice</strong> projects for <strong>8 targets and 68 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Travis Kelce</strong> (TE, 6 targets, 46 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>DEN \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>DEN are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (43% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p><strong>J.K. Dobbins</strong> (13 carries, 62 yards) projects to his baseline workload \u2014 no strong script push either way in a close game.</p><p><strong>Courtland Sutton</strong> projects for <strong>6 targets and 50 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Evan Engram</strong> (TE, 3 targets, 20 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays 